# BookYourSurf.com - AI Assistant Rules

## Project Overview

This is a Nuxt 3 surf business discovery platform built with Vue 3, TypeScript, and SCSS. The app helps users find surf spots, shops, guides, coaching, and photography services in Bali.

## Tech Stack

- **Framework**: Nuxt 3 with Vue 3
- **Language**: TypeScript
- **Styling**: SCSS with custom variables
- **Maps**: Google Maps API with Leaflet
- **Validation**: Regle (Vue validation library)
- **Package Manager**: Yarn
- **Linting**: ESLint with Nuxt config
- **Formatting**: Prettier

## Code Style & Standards

### Vue Components

- Use Composition API with `<script setup>`
- Follow PascalCase for component names
- Use kebab-case for component files
- Always define TypeScript interfaces for props and data
- Use single-file components (.vue) with template, script, and style sections
- Keep components focused and single-purpose

### TypeScript

- Define interfaces in `/types/types.ts`
- Use strict typing for all props, emits, and data
- Import types explicitly when needed
- Follow the existing enum pattern for SurfBusinessType

### SCSS Styling

- Use SCSS variables from `/assets/styles/variables/`
- Follow BEM methodology for CSS classes
- Use the existing color palette and design tokens
- Import variables using the configured SCSS setup
- Keep styles scoped to components when possible
- Use the established spacing and sizing patterns
- **Use minimal CSS - do as little styling as possible**

### File Organization

- Components are organized by feature in `/components/`
- Common components go in `/components/common/`
- Feature-specific components in their respective folders
- Icons in `/assets/icons/` as SVG files
- Images in `/public/images/` organized by category
- Styles in `/assets/styles/` with modular SCSS structure

## Development Guidelines

### Component Structure

```vue
<template>
  <!-- Use semantic HTML -->
</template>

<script setup lang="ts">
// Define interfaces
interface Props {
  // typed props
}

// Define props with defaults
const props = withDefaults(defineProps<Props>(), {
  // defaults
})

// Define emits
const emit = defineEmits<{
  // typed emits
}>()

// Composition logic
</script>

<style lang="scss" scoped>
// Component-specific styles using SCSS variables
</style>
```

### Naming Conventions

- **Files**: kebab-case (e.g., `surf-business-list.vue`)
- **Components**: PascalCase (e.g., `SurfBusinessList`)
- **Variables**: camelCase (e.g., `SurfBusinesss`)
- **CSS Classes**: kebab-case (e.g., `surf-business-list`)
- **Constants**: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `SURF_SPOT`)

### Google Maps Integration

- Use the `useGoogleMaps` composable for map functionality
- Follow the existing pattern for map components
- Handle loading states and error cases
- Use the configured Google Maps API key from runtime config

### Data Management

- Use composables for data fetching and state management
- Follow the existing pattern in `/composables/`
- Mock data should be in `/mocks/` directory
- Use TypeScript interfaces for all data structures

## Code Quality

### Linting & Formatting

- Follow ESLint rules (configured in `eslint.config.mjs`)
- Use Prettier for code formatting
- Run `yarn lint:fix` before committing
- Run `yarn format` for consistent formatting

### Performance

- Use lazy loading for images and components when appropriate
- Optimize bundle size by importing only needed dependencies
- Follow Vue 3 best practices for reactivity
- Use proper key attributes in v-for loops

### Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML elements
- Include proper ARIA labels where needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Maintain proper color contrast ratios

## Project-Specific Patterns

### Surf Business Components

- Use the `SurfBusiness` interface for type safety
- Follow the existing filter and list patterns
- Implement proper rating display with `ItemRating` component
- Use the established map integration patterns

### Form Components

- Use the Regle validation library
- Follow the existing form component patterns
- Implement proper error handling and validation messages
- Use the `ValidationError` component for error display

### Navigation

- Use the existing `Navigation` component
- Follow the established routing patterns
- Implement proper active state handling

## Git Workflow

- Work on the `develop` branch
- Create feature branches for new features
- Use descriptive commit messages
- Test changes before committing
- Follow the existing file organization patterns

## Environment Setup

- Use Yarn as the package manager
- Set up environment variables for Google Maps API
- Follow the existing development server setup
- Use the configured build and deployment scripts

## Common Commands

- `yarn dev` - Start development server
- `yarn build` - Build for production
- `yarn lint` - Run linting
- `yarn format` - Format code
- `yarn generate` - Generate static site

## When Making Changes

1. Always read existing code to understand patterns
2. Follow the established component structure
3. Use existing SCSS variables and design tokens
4. Maintain TypeScript type safety
5. Test changes in the development environment
6. Follow the existing naming conventions
7. Keep components focused and reusable
8. Update types when adding new data structures

## Code Review Checklist

- [ ] TypeScript types are properly defined
- [ ] SCSS follows the established variable system
- [ ] Minimal CSS approach is followed
- [ ] Component follows Vue 3 Composition API patterns
- [ ] Proper error handling is implemented
- [ ] Accessibility considerations are addressed
- [ ] Performance implications are considered
- [ ] Code follows the established naming conventions
- [ ] No console.log statements in production code
